'''Dragan Bender''' (born November 17, 1997) is a Croatian professional [[basketball]] player for [[Maccabi Tel Aviv B.C.|Maccabi Tel Aviv]] of the [[Israeli Basketball Super League]] (BSL). He stands 7&nbsp;ft 1 in (2.16 m) and plays the [[power forward (basketball)|power forward]] and [[center (basketball)|center]] positions. He has been widely considered one of the top international prospects for the [[2016 NBA draft]], and is consistently considered to be a top 3, if not top 5 selection throughout the process. Although originally from [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]], Bender represents [[Croatia men's national basketball team|Croatia]] in international competition, with experience in the [[FIBA Europe]] junior tournaments. Before playing in Israel, he competed with multiple teams in Croatia and in [[Nikola Vujčić]]'s academy.

== Early life ==
Bender was born on November 17, 1997 in [[Čapljina]], [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]], a city on the Croatian border, and he later moved to [[Split, Croatia]].<ref name="grinberg">{{cite web|last1=Leo|first1=Phil|title=Dragan Bender: Future NBA Star is REAL Talent|url=http://www.grinbergnews.com/dragan-bender-nba-star-talent/|website=Grinberg News|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> At 12 years of age, he started playing at [[Nikola Vujčić]]'s basketball academy as a [[point guard]].<ref name=jpost/> Vujčić was a former [[Maccabi Tel Aviv B.C.|Maccabi Tel Aviv]] star and became Bender's guardian.<ref name="br">{{cite web|last1=Pick|first1=David|title=Meet 17-Year-Old European Dragan Bender, Who Could Be 2016's Kristaps Porzingis|url=http://bleacherreport.com/articles/2573742-meet-17-year-old-european-dragan-bender-who-could-be-2016s-kristaps-porzingis|website=[[Bleacher Report]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> At the academy, Bender became a more versatile player and he "learned how to play all the positions on the court."<ref name="fastbreak">{{cite web|last1=Wonsover|first1=Michael|title=International Prospects Bender and Gentile Impress in American Tour|url=http://www.todaysfastbreak.com/nba-draft/bender-gentile-impress-american-tour/|website=Today's Fastbreak|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> Dragan played there along with his older brother, Ivan.<ref name="si">{{cite web|last1=Fischer|first1=Jake|title=Croatian Dragan Bender at epicenter of historic Maccabi-Milano rivalry tour|url=http://www.si.com/nba/2015/09/28/dragan-bender-nba-draft-croatia-maccabi-tel-aviv-olimpia-milano-dario-saric|website=[[Sports Illustrated]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> Bender also played with the [[KK Split]] youth teams at this time, and he appeared for the Croatian under-16 national basketball team.<ref name="yahoo">{{cite web|last1=Givony|first1=Jonathan|title=European Dispatch: Getting to know Dragan Bender|url=http://sports.yahoo.com/news/european-dispatch--getting-to-know-dragan-bender-195451386.html|website=[[Yahoo! Sports]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> He idolized and modeled his game after [[Toni Kukoč]], a former Croatian [[National Basketball Association|NBA]] player, while growing up and watched his old game tape.<ref name=grinberg/><ref name="jpost">{{cite web|last1=Sinai|first1=Allon|title=Mac TA's Bender braces for added attention in next step en route to NBA|url=http://www.jpost.com/Israel-News/Sports/Sinai-says-Mac-TAs-Bender-braces-for-added-attention-in-next-step-en-route-to-NBA-421125|website=[[The Jerusalem Post]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> He mostly watched [[Euroleague]] games but would sometimes tune into the NBA as well. One of his favorite Euroleague players was [[Šarūnas Jasikevičius]].<ref name=si/>

== Professional career ==

=== Croatia (2012–2014) ===
Bender made his professional debut at 15 years of age with [[KK Split]]. He played only a total of six minutes and added two points and one rebound.<ref name=yahoo/><ref name="eurobasket">{{cite web|title=Dragan Bender player profile|url=http://basketball.eurobasket.com/player/Dragan_Bender/Maccabi_Fox_Tel-Aviv/242116|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> For the following season, Bender remained in Croatia and signed with [[KK Kaštela]], who played in the country's second-best league.<ref name="euroleague">{{cite web|title=Bender, Dragan|url=http://www.euroleague.net/competition/players/showplayer?pcode=004549&seasoncode=JT12#!biography|website=''[[Euroleague Basketball]]''|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> With his help, the team was promoted to the [[A-1 Liga]], the top league in Croatia.<ref name="kastela">{{cite web|title=Bender zakucavanjem odveo Kaštelane na prag A1 lige|url=http://www.kastela.org/kastela/udruge/135-novosti/21402-kosarka-bender-zakucavanjem-odveo-kastelane-na-prag-a1-lige|website=''Kastela.org''|accessdate=13 March 2016|language=Croatian}}</ref> Most notably, he made a game-winning shot with three seconds remaining against [[KK Gorica]] to help his team secure the promotion.<ref name=kastela/> Later, in February 2014, Bender joined the [[KK Cedevita]] junior team for the [[Euroleague Basketball Next Generation Tournament]]. He put up 21 points, 9 rebounds, and 6 assists vs the junior team of the Serbian club [[KK Mega Basket]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Dragan Bender Game Logs|url=http://basketball.realgm.com/player/Dragan-Bender/GameLogs/41582/International/2014|website=[[RealGM]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref>

=== Israel (2014–present) ===
In 2014, Bender signed a seven-year contract with [[Maccabi Tel Aviv B.C.|Maccabi Tel Aviv]], a team that played in the [[Israeli Basketball Premier League]] and the [[Euroleague Basketball|Euroleague]].<ref name="future">{{cite web|last1=O'Donnell|first1=Ricky|title=The future of basketball looks a lot like Croatia's Dragan Bender|url=http://www.sbnation.com/college-basketball/2015/6/8/8745739/dragan-bender-nba-draft-2016-profile|website=[[SB Nation]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> He was soon loaned to [[Ironi Ramat Gan]] of the [[Liga Leumit (basketball)|Liga Leumit]], the second-tier league in Israel. Bender would spend the 2014–15 season with Ironi.<ref>{{cite web|title=2015 adidas Eurocamp: Day One|url=http://www.draftexpress.com/article/2015-adidas-Eurocamp-Day-One-5059|website=DraftExpress|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> He made his debut on October 21, 2014, posting 13 points, 13 rebounds, and 4 assists vs [[A.S. Ramat HaSharon|Ramat HaSharon]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Ironi Ramat Gan - Ramat Hasharon 87-83|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2014/1021_437_903.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> He recorded another [[double (basketball)|double-double]] on December 12, with 23 points and 12 rebounds in a win over [[Maccabi Ra'anana]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Maccabi Raanana - Ironi Ramat Gan 73-88|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2014/1202_10044_437.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> Bender scored a season-high 25 points as his team defeated [[Barak Netanya B.C.|Barak Netanya]] by one point on January 1, 2015.<ref>{{cite web|title=Netanya - Ironi Ramat Gan 95-96|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2015/0101_1630_437.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref> After 28 games, he finished the season averaging 9.7 points, 7.4 rebounds, 2.6 assists, and 1.1 blocks.<ref name=eurobasket/> As the season came to a close, Bender returned to Maccabi Tel Aviv, where he would continue his career for the following season.<ref>{{cite web|title=Maccabi Tel Aviv out to avenge recent flops as BSL season tips off|url=http://www.jpost.com/Israel-News/Sports/Maccabi-Tel-Aviv-out-to-avenge-recent-flops-as-BSL-season-tips-off-421411|website=[[The Jerusalem Post]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref>

Bender debuted for Maccabi Tel Aviv in the Israeli Premier League on October 11, 2015, with 5 points, 3 rebounds, and 3 blocks in a win over [[Maccabi Kiryat Gat B.C.|Maccabi Kiryat Gat]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Maccabi T-A - Maccabi K/G 89-65|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2015/1011_159_1828.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=6 April 2016}}</ref> He scored double figures for the first time with the team in a rematch with Kiryat Gat, adding 15 points.<ref name=yahoo/><ref>{{cite web|title=Maccabi K/G - Maccabi T-A 63-88|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2015/1220_1828_159.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=6 April 2016}}</ref> Bender scored a season-high 16 points on December 31, 2015, as his team defeated [[Hapoel Holon]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Maccabi T-A - Hapoel Holon 93-75|url=http://www.eurobasket.com/boxScores/Israel/2015/1231_159_436.asp|website=[[Eurobasket.com]]|accessdate=6 April 2016}}</ref> During his second season with Maccabi Tel Aviv, Bender would be named a foreign All-Star for the event. He'd also help Tel Aviv repeat as champions in the [[Israeli League Cup]] by beating [[Maccabi Ashdod B.C.|Maccadi Ashdod]] 83-75. Bender would end up being one of 13 international underclassmen (and one of three different Croatians) to officially take part in the [[2016 NBA Draft]]. In the event a team in the draft would want him to play in the NBA immediately<!--likely either the Boston Celtics or the Phoenix Suns at this time, although the Minnesota Timberwolves can also be possibilities as well-->, both Bender and the team would have to pay a combined total of $1.3 million to buyout his contract in Israel.<ref>[http://www.draftexpress.com/article/Dragan-Bender-Buyout-Numbers-Revealed-5498/ Dragan Bender Buyout Numbers Revealed]</ref>

== International career ==
Bender competed for the [[Croatia national basketball team|Croatian national team]] at the [[2012 FIBA Europe Under-16 Championship]] in Latvia and Lithuania. Through five contests, he averaged 2.8 points and 1.4 rebounds in 5.6 minutes per game.<ref>{{cite web|title=Dragan Bender|url=http://www.fibaeurope.com/compID_YUjW-7-FJ,kK9s431Lyr41.season_2012.roundID_8814.playerID_100891.teamID_2168.html|website=''FIBAEurope.com''|publisher=[[FIBA]]|accessdate=6 April 2016}}</ref> In 2015, Bender was called up Croatia for the [[2015 FIBA Under-19 World Championship]] in [[Crete|Crete, Greece]], but he was forced to withdraw because the team's players were obliged to wear [[Jordan Brand]] clothes and shoes due to a sponsorship agreement. Bender had signed a similar agreement with his sponsor [[Adidas]], which prevented him to wear other brands.<ref name="sneakers">{{cite web|title=Just for Kicks: Rising star Bender leaves FIBA U19s over sneaker dispute|url=http://www.si.com/nba/2015/07/02/dragan-bender-croatia-nba-fiba-sneakers-adidas-jordan-brand|website=[[Sports Illustrated]]|accessdate=13 March 2016}}</ref>

== Player profile ==
Bender has been strongly compared to [[Kristaps Porziņģis]] to the point where NBA executives believe he's a Top 5 selection similar to Porziņģis because of their playing styles, either due to it being a selling point or being a good cover overall.<ref>[http://www.nba.com/2016/news/features/scott_howard_cooper/06/17/knicks-porzingis-having-impact-in-this-years-draft/index.html?ls=iref:nba:draft:headlines Bosnian big man getting deep look for top draft position]</ref> However, while Porziņģis may be the stronger shooter, Bender is consistently labelled as the stronger passer and ball handler by comparison. A general manager in the NBA even commented that his game could be even better than Porziņģis's, saying "His size, the way he plays. He's probably the most talented European I've seen since [[Dirk Nowitzki]]." However, Bender considers his idol, [[Toni Kukoč]], a more acceptable comparison to him.

